    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Barinov scolds Katya for volunteering her molecular show in front of Vika. Meanwhile, Max complains to Kostya that Vika kicked him out for Nagiyev. Listen to the full conversation in Russian first, and then we will walk through every line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Катя, что это сейчас было?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебе что, больше делать нечего?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Куда ты лезешь вперёд отца?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Ты же сам сразу отказался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Мог бы сказать и спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Я спасла тебя от завтрашнего дня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Давай будем вместе, я готов меняться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А она меня сразу выгнала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да ладно, первый раз, что ли?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А знаешь, почему она выгнала?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Потому что она Нагиева ждала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какого ещё Нагиева?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Катя, что это сейчас было?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Katya, what was that just now?' Notice how Russian expresses 'Katya' and 'was' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебе что, больше делать нечего?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have nothing better to do?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'to you' and finishing on 'nothing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Куда ты лезешь вперёд отца?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why are you stepping ahead of your father?' In Russian, the idea of 'where to' flows naturally into 'father' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Ты же сам сразу отказался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You yourself refused right away.' Katya brings out the contrast between 'you' and 'but' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Мог бы сказать и спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You could have said thank you too.' Notice how Russian expresses 'can' and 'thank you' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Я спасла тебя от завтрашнего дня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I saved you from tomorrow.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'day'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Давай будем вместе, я готов меняться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us be together, I am ready to change.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'change'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А она меня сразу выгнала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And she kicked me out right away.' Kostya anchors the line on 'and', while 'kicked out' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да ладно, первый раз, что ли?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, is it the first time?' The grammar packs 'yes' and 'whether' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "А знаешь, почему она выгнала?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And do you know why she kicked me out?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'and' and finishing on 'kicked out'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Потому что она Нагиева ждала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because she was waiting for Nagiyev.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'that is why' before landing on 'waited for'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какого ещё Нагиева?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Which Nagiyev?' The meaning hinges on 'what' working together with 'Nagiyev' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
